    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001071"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.org/dc/terms/description"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Group 3 innate lymphoid cells (ILC3) belong to the family of innate lymphoid cells. They share a high degree of similarity with T lymphocytes, particularly in terms of their transcriptional program and their cytokine production profiles, but lack the antigen-specific receptors that characterize adaptive immune cells. Originating from common lymphoid progenitors, ILC3s are typically found in mucosal tissues and are especially prevalent in the gastrointestinal tract. The grouping of ILC3s originally included lymphoid tissue inducer (LTi) cells, which have more recently been categorized separately.
