        <owl:annotatedTarget>Fibroblasts of the mammary gland, often referred to as mammary fibroblasts, are a specialized type of cell located within the connective tissue of the mammary glands. These cells are integral to the structure and composition of the gland and are one of its primary cell types, specializing in the production of the extracellular matrix and collagen. They are involved in tissue remodeling, shaping the structure and maintaining the integrity of the mammary tissue by providing necessary rigidity and elasticity through their production of structural proteins. 
These cells also play a crucial role in mammary gland development and lactation. During mammary gland development, fibroblasts play a significant role in the induction of branching morphogenesis, which is vital for establishing proper ductal architecture. They also support the immune response within the mammary gland by producing and secreting cytokines and growth factors. 
